Review this new endpoint:
```python
@app.post("/v1/orders")
def create_order(req):
    return Order.create(
        items=req.json["items"],
        customer_id=req.json["customer_id"],
        internal_priority_score=req.json.get("internal_priority_score"),  # NEW: lets caller set our internal fulfillment ranking
        source_channel=req.json.get("source_channel", "api"),
    )
```
No product or fulfillment team requested exposing `internal_priority_score` to API consumers; it was added because the field already existed on the internal `Order` model.

Review this new endpoint:
```python
@app.post("/v1/payments")
def create_payment(req):
    try:
        return process_payment(req.json)
    except InsufficientFundsError:
        return {"error": "nope, cant do that"}, 400
    except CardDeclinedError as e:
        return {"error": str(e)}, 402
    except Exception as e:
        return {"error": repr(e)}, 500
```

Review this new endpoint:
```python
@app.get("/v2/customers")
def list_customers(req):
    return {"items": [c.to_dict() for c in Customer.query.limit(50)], "next_page_token": ...}
```
Every other collection endpoint in this API (`/v2/orders`, `/v2/invoices`, `/v2/products`) returns `{"data": [...], "cursor": ...}` — this is the only one using `items`/`next_page_token` instead of the established `data`/`cursor` naming.

Review this new endpoint:
```python
@app.post("/v1/events")
def create_event(req):
    timestamp = req.json.get("timestamp")
    if timestamp and not isinstance(timestamp, str):
        timestamp = str(timestamp)   # silently coerces int/float timestamps to string
    return Event.create(timestamp=timestamp, payload=req.json.get("payload"))
```

